# EXP-0027 postmortem and offline semantic review

New evidence after EXP-0027 close:
- colour-freshness-motion-EXP-0027.log (VP-labelled hashes and preregistered failure).
- _local/scratch/colour_sem/sheet_E_roles.png and proof_picked_static.png plus metrics.json,
  decoded from the same run's original fmt26 dumps.

Corrections and constraints:
1. Picked idx0 is a complete render-resolution world image, not a mask. idx1 is nearly
   the same image with a different gain. Do not switch to idx4 merely because it has HDR
   values: the earlier B1c evidence says the colour buffer already contains tone mapping,
   and idx4 is an HDR sky/remote layer with clipped terrain, not the complete final world.
2. The run1/run2 picked images have zero measured pixel shift; the 2.56% byte change is
   not proof of semantic freshness or camera-visible motion. The near-stale experiment
   remains failed under its own threshold, but resource identity is still unresolved.
3. Existing deep dependency scans are not exhaustive proof of native-resource absence:
   their global DFS budget can be consumed by module pointers. A flat, read-only qword
   comparison against the live native-resource inventory has not been tried.
4. Existing runtime objects distinguish ExternalImage color/motion from TransientImage
   history/output by vtable. The semantic source is already available as taau arg10;
   the missing step is only dependency -> native resource identity.

Next single unknown:
Can a bounded flat scan of the InputFinalColor dependency and its immediate objects find
an exact pointer match in the current native resource inventory?

This is a read-only identity experiment. It does not change the selected colour, DLSS,
TAAU suppression, exposure, or any GPU resource. A hit enables semantic binding; no hit
justifies investigating mce::Texture/bgfx resolution rather than trying more candidates.
